      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, sorry about that! I got a little carried away with the goblin imagery, but don't worry, I'm just trying to keep the study session lively. You're absolutely right to focus on the physics, and I'm glad you're asking for clarification on that tricky part of the formula.\n\nLet's break down why frequency ($f$) being in the denominator makes capacitive reactance ($X_C$) decrease as frequency increases. Think of a capacitor as a gatekeeper that gets more impatient the faster you try to push it open. In a DC circuit, the frequency is zero, so the gate is fully locked (infinite reactance), and no current flows. But in an AC circuit, the voltage is constantly changing direction.\n\nWhen the frequency is low, the voltage changes slowly, giving the capacitor plenty of time to charge up and build up a strong electric field that resists the flow of electrons. However, when the frequency is high, the voltage flips back and forth so quickly that the capacitor never has time to fully charge up before the voltage reverses again. It's like trying to fill a bucket with a hose that turns off and on rapidly; you can't fill it up, so the resistance to",
